issues as an employment or  inclusive growth or investments or what  have you of course were lacking and as a  result in a place like Egypt where  Egyptian society is conservative and is  religious but that same society does not  want anyone to tell it how to practice  that religion or that conservatism what  people in Egypt and elsewhere in the  Arab world wanted was to add for for any  political party in power to address  their everyday problems and when that  that when that did not happen the same  street in Egypt that brought to Islam is  to power went out to the street again to  deny that power of course in Egypt that  was mitigated and in my view that was  very unfortunate by the intervention by  the military the intervention by the  military robbed the street that power  and that very important message that it  sent to anybody who wants to exercise  you know exclusionist behavior the  minute  the minute military intervened it  mitigated that message hugely in my view  but there is another message in the  making in another country that is not  receiving much attention these days  probably because it's not it does not  have the same turmoil or the same  killings that we are witnessing in Egypt  or Syria the country where it all  started Tunisia can potentially send an  extremely important message to the rest  of the Arab world today the process in  Tunisia is far from smooth but it is  moving and the Tunisians are almost you  know have almost decided on a new  constitution by consensus and are on  their way to deciding to have new  elections in few months all the polls  today in Tunisia all the polls show that  the Islamists support has gone down to  about 13% which if you translate it into  seats means that is if if the elections  are held tomorrow the Islamists will  lose another in particular will lose  from having 90 seats in the old  Constituent Assembly to having about 70  or 65 seats in the new assembly and neda  Tunis the secular party that was only  formed two days to two years ago whose  leader is 87 years old and his accused  of being part of the old regime has  twenty-two percent in the polls today in  other words if it's still an if but if  the elections go ahead and if the  results of the polls stand and I think  they will we will have in for the first  time in the Arab world a shattering of  the notion that Islamists will not leave  power  once they come to it and if that goes on  then I think this scare tactic that Arab  governments have used all this time to  say that we are the best you know the  better of the two evils and and that you  know if you don't support us then the  Islamics will come in that notion would  be shattered if indeed we see Tunisia  going ahead as I hope it would Islamist  have lost their holiness in my view they  have lost their holiness they can no  longer today any Islamist party that  goes to elections with the slogan Islam  is the solution will will will not have  almost any support from the electorate  Islam is the solution was a good slogan  when the Islamists were outside power  now that they are inside power Islam as  a solution is not enough to solve the  people's daily problems you would think  that I'm you know very hard on the  Islamists but I'm equally hard on the  secular forces as well unfortunately the  emerging secular forces in the Arab  world today are more liberal than  democratic in fact they are downright  undemocratic and secular forces in Egypt  have shown this you know very clearly at  the first instance at the first instance  where they could see an opportunity for  them to win by other than electoral  means they chose to align with the  military practicing exactly the  exclusion is the same exclusionist  policies that they have accused the  Islamists of doing the main criticism by  secular force  of the islamists where that these people  will come to power and deny it to  anybody else and what is happening in  Egypt today is exactly the same except  that the secular forces want to come to  power and deny the right of all others  to operate that is not going to work  neither the exclusionist practices by  the Islamists nor the exclusionist  practices by the secular forces in my  view will work unfortunately until the  Arab world finds this out it's going to  take probably decades what I see  happening in the Arab world  unfortunately is a battle between the  secular elements and the Islamist  elements not a battle for pluralism and  in such a zero-sum battle the winner  takes all whoever wins that button is  going to be the new dictator of the Arab  world when the Islamists win there will  be the new dictators when the secular  forces when they will be the new  dictators as well but fortunately in my  view what the Egyptians have shown with  20 people we can differ on the numbers  with many millions of people going to  the street what the Egyptians have shown  is that from now on they are not going  to allow any exclusionist force to  dominate and that in my view is  extremely important for the process of  the development of pluralistic systems  in the Arab world even if we are only at  the beginning of such a process a  process that is going to take a long  time another issue I think that has  become extremely important to look at in  in the Arab world is the issue of  national identities look at what is  happening in North Africa and look at  what is happening in the mushroom with  all the problems that North Africa has  in Egypt in Tunisia in Libya  and in Morocco with all the these  problems at least in Egypt and Tunisia  you do have a sense of a national  identity Egyptians think of themselves  as Egyptians first and they have done so  for not just centuries maybe for  millennia long before the modern  nation-state was created they are more  or less homogeneous societies and  therefore while they will still take  maybe a decade or two until they you  know set themselves on a track to  stability and prosperity they will face  and relatively much smoother transition  than what the mushroom of the Arab world  the eastern part of the Arab world will  in the eastern part of the Arab world  most of the countries are creations of  the sykes-picot agreement in 1916 where  you know boundaries were artificially  drawn were different ethnic religious  groups were put together in countries  without any basically common sense of  identity beyond the fact that they were  all not all but mostly Arabs and mostly  Muslim and so in in in Syria in in the  last hundred years that separate us from  sykes-picot we have not seen any country  use those hundred years to forge a true  sense of national identity so that  Assyrian you know thinks of himself or  herself first as a Syrian not as a Druze  or a Christian or occurred or an Alawite  or a Sunni or what have you and the mush  wreck of the Arab world is paying the  price today in Iraq in Lebanon in Syria  and probably in other part of the world  including my own if people are not very  wise about how to treat the issue of  national  entity unless the Arab world understands  that national identities have to trump  all sub identities in my own country  Jordan is Jordan ins and Palestinians  unless it understands that we are  setting ourselves on a course which is  not far from what is happening today in  Syria in Iraq and in Lebanon and that  again brings me to the issue that is  very dear to my heart and one that I  have been talking about for some time  the Arab world is badly in need to  revisit and change its educational  policy when we talk about education  reform in the Arab world unfortunately  we still talk about putting computers in  schools we still talk about building new  schools we still talk about issues that  are important but are largely issues of  quantity the quality of education is  never talked about we never talked about  a system of values that on you know that  appreciates diversity tolerance  different point of view that teacher  skills that's critical thinking research  communication all the issues that are  essential to developing pluralistic  societies because democracy will not  thrive except in a culture that accepts  diversity and treats it as a strength  and not as a weakness in the Arab world  even though we're very diverse and even  though we talk about diversity in  practice diversity is a bad word durable  everybody is supposed to think alike  along the common cause and put aside  their differences  what's the way ahead with all these  problems well one notion is that the  transformational process that is going  on might have a better chance in  countries that have not undergone  transitions yet in the Arab world if  their leadership our wives the argument  goes that reform from above still might  offer the best solution because it is it  is a process that ensures a gradual  transition to democracy without applying  a lot of shocks to the system that of  course that is easier said than done  because ironically most of the countries  that have not undergone transitions in  the Arab world are the monarchies of the  arbor both the rich monarchies of the  Gulf and the poor monarchies of Morocco  and Jordan and how do you argue to the  leadership's in these countries that the  only way to keep their power from now on  is to share it that argument is very  difficult to internalize so while on one  hand it might offer the best chance of a  smooth transition the reality on the  ground is that none of these none of  these countries are setting themselves  on a course to gradual but serious  reform the Gulf countries the rich  monarchies are attempting to deal with  this through financial handouts  literally and they have been able to  maybe succeed at least at least until  now in slowing down the process although  you see that in Bahrain it didn't you  see that in Kuwait there are serious  some serious opposition to the monitored  to the America and even in Saudi Arabia  I think after King Abdullah leaves the  scene you're going to see some some  trouble as well but in the poor  monarchies of the  world in Morocco and Jordan the approach  so far has been to offer cosmetic reform  reform that does not really result in a  serious reform process and I'll speak  about what I mean when I talk about the  serious reform process hoping that you  know the people of these countries would  look at the countries around them would  get scared of what is happening around  them and would no longer call for reform  measures in their countries that in my  view is very short-sighted the problems  do not disappear just because of the  turmoil around them and people will  always find a way to voice their  frustration if they see that their  leadership might not be serious about a  referral process what is a successful  reform process from above what elements  should it entail in my view if it is to  be successful well the first is power  sharing you cannot talk about the  serious reform process if it does not  result in power sharing if it does not  result in strengthening the legislative  and the judicial branches at the expense  of the executive which has been  extremely dominant in the Arab world for  decades so power-sharing developing what  what you Americans call a system of  checks and balances where no power  really dominates any other and were you  you make sure that there are limits to  any power exercised by any branch on the  economic front I think it must entail  and moving away from the rentier system  that the Arab world has witnessed for so  many decades oil has been as much occurs  as a blessing in the Arab world oil has  me has meant that because money  literally grew on the ground  productivity was killed a patron system  was put in place where the governments  were able to basically exercise what I  call  royalties for loyalties so you give a  layer around you certain privileges in  return for the royalty that is not a  system that produces jobs that is not a  system that can deal with the huge  economic issues of the Arab world the  huge unemployment problem of the Arab  world where unemployment in the Arab  world is double the world's average you  are not going to do it through a system  that kills productivity and and as a  result we need to move away to a  merit-based system in order to be able  to do that and the third element is  inclusion again we speak of inclusion  but the Arab world you know if Sunnis  and Shiites cannot argue agree among  themselves and they are both you know  part of the same religion how are we to  are to agree on differences between  Islam and Christianity or between men  and women between Kurds and Arabs how  are we going to do that and so a system  that does not work for inclusion and a  sense of national identity cannot be a  serious reform process and the last  element is education as i said the arab  world has to has to shift the emphasis  from the quantity of education to the  quality and it does not need new  resources they are bored already spends  five percent of GDP on education which  is equivalent to what OECD countries  spend but it is how it spends it rather  than the amount that it spends and  finally I spoke for too long a word  about the u.s. ok what is the u.s. role  in all this what are you guys to do  about this region the short answer is  not much this is a homegrown process and  it's not a process that anybody can  affect in any significant way I'm not  suggesting that the US has not  play but I am suggesting that the US has  to be realistic about what kind of a  role it wants to play and what can it do  and not do in a process that will have  to take its course I would be happy  personally with you as encouraging the  process and not picking winners and  losers and let the region do that on  itself what the US has done in the past  is it has sided itself with winners or  losers and only to find out that people  excited with turned out to be not as  popular or stable as the u.s. thought  them to be and so my bottom line is you  know we are just the witnessing the  first of a very very this first chapter  in an extremely long book people need to  be patient I know it's not a sexy  recommendation but there are no shot no  shortcuts to democracy or stability  anywhere in the world and those who do  really want a pluralistic Arab world  will have to do it on the ground from  now on the way the Islamists have done  it before the secular elements have for  a long time accepted to be elitist  accepted to be preaching from behind  podiums maybe in the same way that I'm  doing now rather than working on the  ground developing networks developing  programs pushing for pluralistic  societies the process that is going to  take decades and that is why I believe  that if there is hope in the Arab world  it must lie with the new generation a  new generation from the Islamists and  the secular elements who believe in both  in both a civil state and in a democracy  at the same time and so that is is  entirely possible but it's going to take  time thank you very much
 

